---
/* What is measured, in plain words.
 *
 * Every claim on this page is a claim about code in this repository, and
 * checks/site-beacon.js checks each one against a real browser loading the real
 * build. A privacy page that describes an intention rather than an
 * implementation is the same failure as a config file that validates and does
 * nothing.
 *
 * ── this page was wrong, and the check could not see it ───────────────
 * It used to say "no external analytics — nothing on a report page makes a
 * request to anyone but us", while Vercel Web Analytics was enabled in
 * astro.config.mjs and shipping on all nine built pages, this one included.
 *
 * The check meant to enforce that compared request HOST, and Vercel's script is
 * served from a same-origin path the platform proxies — so it passed locally
 * and would have passed in production too. It now compares destination.
 *
 * Which means there are two measurement systems described below, not one, and
 * they behave differently: ours honours Do Not Track, Vercel's does not. Saying
 * so is the whole job of this page.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../lib/config";
---

<Base
  title={`Privacy — ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description="What this site measures, what it does not, and how to stop it."
>
  <header class="top no-print">
    <a class="home" href="/">{SITE_NAME}</a>
  </header>

  <main class="article">
    <div class="masthead">
      <h1>What this site measures</h1>
      <p class="dek">
        Short version: a random number in your browser, which pages you looked at, and how
        far down you got. No advertising, nothing sold, and nothing that identifies you by
        name. Two systems do the counting — ours, and Vercel's — and they are described
        separately below because they do not behave the same way.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Our own measurement</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A random identifier</strong> this site generates and keeps in your browser's
        local storage. It is not derived from anything about you — it identifies a browser, not
        a person, and clearing site data genuinely resets it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Which pages you opened</strong>, how far you scrolled, roughly how long the tab
        was actually visible, and whether you clicked through to the research portal.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Where you arrived from</strong> — the referring site and any campaign tags on
        the link — recorded once, on your first visit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A two-letter country</strong>, worked out at the network edge, and a coarse
        device class (phone, tablet or desktop) based on window width.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What our measurement does not record</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your IP address.</strong> The country is resolved at the network edge before the
        request reaches our code, so the address never arrives at the part of the system that
        could store it. We do not write it down anywhere.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Your user-agent string</strong>, or any browser or device fingerprint.</li>
      <li><strong>Your name or email</strong>, unless you type one into the form below a report.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Anything sold or shared.</strong> There are no advertising pixels, no data
        brokers, and nothing here is passed to anyone for marketing.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Vercel Web Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      This site is hosted by <strong>Vercel</strong>, and Vercel's own page-view counter is
      switched on. It is our hosting provider acting as a processor for us — it is not an
      advertising network and it does not follow you to other sites — but it is a second system
      and it is not the same as ours, so it is worth being precise:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It records the page, the referrer and any campaign tags, and derives a
        <strong>country and region from your IP</strong> and a <strong>browser, OS and device
        from your user-agent</strong>, on Vercel's servers.
      </li>
      <li>
        It combines those into a rotating hash to count returning visitors. Vercel states it
        does not store the IP address itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It does not honour Do Not Track.</strong> Ours does; this one does not, and we
        cannot make it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to stop it</h2>
    <p>
      Turn on <strong>Do Not Track</strong> or <strong>Global Privacy Control</strong> in your
      browser. <strong>Our</strong> measurement checks for both <em>before</em> it generates an
      identifier or touches storage — so with either set you are not merely un-reported by us,
      you are un-recorded. Clearing site data removes the identifier and the next visit starts a
      new one.
    </p>
    <p>
      That does <em>not</em> stop Vercel's counter, which has no such switch. A content blocker
      that blocks <code>/_vercel/insights/</code> does, and so does blocking scripts on this
      domain — neither breaks anything you came here to read.
    </p>

    <h2>Email</h2>
    <p>
      Entirely optional, and nothing on this site is behind it. Every report is free to read
      whether or not you sign up. Ticking the box records that you agreed to be emailed; leaving
      it records that you did not, and nothing is sent either way until the address has been
      confirmed by a link sent to it. We also record which report you signed up from, so we know
      which work was worth reading.
    </p>

    <h2>Why we measure at all</h2>
    <p>
      This site is free and is meant to be found. Knowing which reports get read, and how far,
      is how we decide what to write next — which is a better arrangement for a reader than
      putting the work behind a signup form and measuring that instead.
    </p>

    <p class="fine">
      Anonymous measurement is processed on the basis of legitimate interest; the optional email
      is consent. Vercel processes hosting and analytics data on our behalf under their data
      processing terms. If you want the identifier or an address removed, ask and it will be.
    </p>

    <p class="back"><a href="/">← Back to the site</a></p>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .top {
    max-width: 1080px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 14px 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair);
  }
  .home {
    font-weight: 640;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  .article {
    padding: 34px 0 72px;
  }
  .masthead {
    display: grid;
    gap: 9px;
    margin-bottom: 26px;
  }
  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(27px, 1.6rem + 1.8vw, 40px);
    line-height: 1.13;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    font-weight: 660;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .dek {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1rem + 0.3vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--body);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  h2 {
    margin: 30px 0 8px;
    font-size: 17px;
    font-weight: 640;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    display: grid;
    gap: 7px;
  }
  li,
  p {
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .article > p {
    margin: 0 0 1em;
  }
  strong {
    font-weight: 650;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .fine {
    margin-top: 28px;
    padding-top: 14px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--small);
  }
  .back {
    margin-top: 22px;
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }
</style>
